# Local dev environment for the API server (`npm run dev`).
#
# Copy this file to `server/.env` (gitignored) and set ENERGY_DB_PATH to the
# absolute path of your local energy_dashboard.db. The dev server loads
# `server/.env` automatically via `tsx watch --env-file-if-exists=.env`.
#
# NOT used in Docker/production: there ENERGY_DB_PATH comes from the container
# (docker/.env -> docker-compose.yml), and the server runs the built bundle
# (`npm start`), which does not read this file.
#
# Path is machine-specific. On the able workstation the replica lives here:
ENERGY_DB_PATH=C:/Code/able/data/energy_dashboard.db

# Base URL of the OTHER environment's dashboard API, for GET /api/ops/status/combined
# (ABL-238's acceptance/prod status page). Prod's peer is acceptance and
# acceptance's peer is prod — set the one this process is NOT running as.
# Unset means "not configured": the status page shows that distinctly from an
# unreachable peer, and does not attempt an outbound request. In Docker/production
# this comes from docker/.env -> docker-compose.yml, same as ENERGY_DB_PATH above.
# OPS_PEER_URL=http://192.168.86.36:3001

# Ops-status snapshot capture (ABL-288), the trend + disk-headroom half of the
# status page. Every OPS_SNAPSHOT_INTERVAL_MINUTES the server appends one line
# describing GET /api/ops/status/combined to a JSONL file — its OWN file, never
# a table in the shared database, which belongs to energy-data-gathering.
#
# All four are optional; the defaults below are what runs if you set none of
# them. Capture is ON by default (unlike the two DB-writing schedulers, which
# are gated on explicit env vars) because it only ever writes this one file,
# and a trend that needs a flag flipped before it starts accumulating is a
# trend nobody has when they first need it.
#
# The default path is alongside ENERGY_DB_PATH, so by default a new
# `ops-status-snapshots.jsonl` appears in that directory — set OPS_SNAPSHOT_PATH
# to put it somewhere else. An unwritable path is not fatal: capture is logged
# as failed and /api/ops/status/history reports the error instead of drawing a
# flat line at zero.
# OPS_SNAPSHOT_ENABLED=true
# OPS_SNAPSHOT_INTERVAL_MINUTES=15
# OPS_SNAPSHOT_RETENTION_DAYS=14
# OPS_SNAPSHOT_PATH=C:/Code/able/data/ops-status-snapshots.jsonl

# --- Ops alert engine (ABL-287) -------------------------------------------
# The scheduled check that notifies when an ops KPI crosses into warn/error and
# again when it recovers. Reads the same verdicts /api/ops/status/combined
# serves, in-process. Notifications go to the log — that is the only channel
# there is, by Board decision; no mail, no credentials, nothing to configure.
#
# On by default. Set to false in a checkout that does not want the log lines
# (it also stops the 5-minute freshness query the check drives).
# OPS_ALERTS_ENABLED=false
#
# How often to evaluate. Default 5 minutes; values below 1 minute or
# unparseable ones fall back to the default rather than spinning the check.
# OPS_ALERT_INTERVAL_MINUTES=5
#
# Where the last-notified state per KPI is persisted, so a restart does not
# re-announce everything already reported. Defaults to
# `ops-alert-state.json` beside ENERGY_DB_PATH. This is NOT a time series —
# ABL-288 owns the readings; this file only records what was last said.
# OPS_ALERT_STATE_PATH=C:/Code/able/data/ops-alert-state.json

# --- Breach watch (ABL-578) -----------------------------------------------
# The scheduled check that reads the /v1 auth-failure tables ABL-530 fills and
# opens a priority:high INCIDENT issue for the CEO when an ABL-524 Tier 1
# signal trips. Until it existed, a credential attack was fully visible in
# storage and seen by nobody.
#
# It runs HERE, in the private process, and not in the /v1 process that owns
# those tables — so that the Paperclip credential below stays out of the
# process ABL-291 may expose. See services/breachWatch/authFailureReader.ts.
#
# NOTHING BELOW IS REQUIRED. With API_KEYS_DB_PATH unset (the normal state of a
# dev checkout, and of any deployment not running /v1) the check reports
# "nothing to watch" once at boot and does nothing further. It never creates,
# writes to or migrates the key store: the handle is readonly.
#
# On by default, like the ops alert engine above.
# BREACH_WATCH_ENABLED=false
#
# How often to look. Default 15 minutes; unparseable or sub-minute values fall
# back to the default rather than spinning the check.
# BREACH_WATCH_INTERVAL_MINUTES=15
#
# WHERE AN ALARM GOES. ABL-524 §6 is a Board decision of 2026-08-22: a detected
# signal opens a priority:high Paperclip issue assigned to the CEO, titled
# `INCIDENT: …`. The channel reads the three PAPERCLIP_* variables below. With
# any of them unset the finding is LOGGED ONLY and nobody is woken — the boot
# line says so in those words, because an alarm that looks delivered and was
# not is worse than no alarm.
# PAPERCLIP_API_URL=http://192.168.86.237:3100
# PAPERCLIP_API_KEY=
# PAPERCLIP_COMPANY_ID=
# PAPERCLIP_PROJECT_ID=
#
# THESE BELONG TO THE PRIVATE PROCESS ONLY. PAPERCLIP_API_KEY is on
# FORBIDDEN_PUBLIC_ENV (server/src/v1/publicEnv.ts, ABL-591): the public /v1
# composition refuses to start if it finds it, because the watcher was put in
# this process precisely so that the process ABL-291 may expose cannot silence
# the alarm that describes an attacker who took it. If the two ever share an
# env file, neutralise it on the public side with `PAPERCLIP_API_KEY=` rather
# than granting it.
#
# The CEO agent. Defaults to the id ABL-578 names; override only if the roster
# changes.
# BREACH_WATCH_ASSIGNEE_AGENT_ID=
#
# Detection windows. The defaults match the `npm run usage -- security:*`
# commands, so an alarm and a hand-run report cannot disagree about the same
# instant. The origin lookback is bounded above by the 90-day scrub.
# BREACH_WATCH_WINDOW_HOURS=24
# BREACH_WATCH_ORIGIN_LOOKBACK_DAYS=30
#
# Idempotency: one open incident per subject per window, updated rather than
# duplicated. A growing incident is commented on at most this often.
# BREACH_WATCH_INCIDENT_WINDOW_HOURS=24
# BREACH_WATCH_UPDATE_INTERVAL_HOURS=6
#
# THE ONE PROVISIONAL NUMBER IN THE WHOLE FEATURE. ABL-524 §2 S3 states the
# enumeration signal as a shape — "many distinct presented prefixes, one source
# IP" — and gives no cutoff, so this one was chosen in ABL-578 and is proposed
# back to ABL-524 for calibration. S2 and S4 carry no threshold at all. An
# incident raised by this rule says PROVISIONAL in its body.
# BREACH_WATCH_MIN_PREFIXES_PER_ORIGIN=10
#
# Where the open-incident record is persisted. Defaults to
# `breach-watch-state.json` beside ENERGY_DB_PATH — deliberately NOT beside
# API_KEYS_DB_PATH, which is the file ABL-524 §0 makes the whole reportable
# surface and which Tier 2 would watch for writes.
# BREACH_WATCH_STATE_PATH=C:/Code/able/data/breach-watch-state.json
